{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Its four pa'amot</b> — as Onkelos renders: \"its four corners,\" and they were set in the upper corners near the cover. \"And two rings on its one side\" — these are the very same four rings mentioned at the beginning of the verse, and the meaning of the verse is: and two of these rings on one side. Thus Rashi explained (Rashi on Exodus 25:12), and he explained well. But I do not know why he said that the rings were in the upper corners near the cover, for the weight was very great, and furthermore it is the way of honor that the Ark should be carried elevated and high above the shoulders of the priests.\n\nIbn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 25:12): I searched through all of Scripture and did not find \"pa'am\" meaning a corner, only a foot — \"How beautiful are your pa'amot\" (Song of Songs 7:2), \"the feet of the poor, the pa'amot of the needy\" (Isaiah 26:6). Therefore I was compelled to explain that the Ark had feet, and he explained that there were eight rings: four lower ones to carry it with, and the upper ones were for beauty. But his words are not correct at all. For if, according to his own words, the pa'am is a foot, Scripture should command that the rings be in the lower corners on which the Ark rests, and it would call the lower sides \"feet.\" For the language of the Holy Scripture grasps all forms according to the likeness of a person, and says of every thing: \"head\" for its upper part and \"foot\" for its lower side. And this is indeed so: the rings were in the lower corners, and the Ark was carried elevated, as I explained above.\n\nBut in my opinion, pa'am is not \"foot,\" but rather a name for a step — \"How beautiful are your pa'amot\" (Song of Songs 7:2): your steps, as in the matter stated in the Talmud (Avodah Zarah 18a): \"How fine are the steps of this young woman.\" And likewise \"Why is it delayed — the pa'am of his chariots?\" (Judges 5:28). And Scripture said here \"its pa'amot\" for the steps of the priests carrying it, alluding to two things: that the rings should be in the corners below, truly close to the base of the Ark, and that the full length of the Ark should separate between the two rings — for the Ark's length ran east to west, and the rings: two on the north side, one at the eastern end and one at the western, and two on the south, and the steps of the priests would go between ring and ring, and in front of them.\n\nAnd in the Mishnah of the Tabernacle they taught: Four rings of gold were fixed in it, two on its north and two on its south, into which they would place the poles, and they would never move from there, and so forth."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:16:32.573518763Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}